{
  "_comment": [
    "Cross-implementation parity cases, as RAW JSON on purpose.",
    "",
    "These must never be generated by one side and handed to the other: a case",
    "authored in JS and serialized loses exactly the distinctions that break",
    "parity (JSON `100.0` becomes `100` on the way out, so the float case can",
    "never appear). Every value below is written literally, the way a foreign",
    "instance might actually put it on the wire, and both implementations read",
    "the same bytes.",
    "",
    "Each entry is fed to the named derivation and the two results must match."
  ],
